  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nAnother warm and windy day has taken shape over the most of the area,\nwith temperatures approaching 70F and winds gusting to 45-50 \nMPH in many locations. The only exception to this has been \npoints north/west of Norfolk, where low clouds have prevented as\nmuch of the 50+ kt 850-mb flow from mixing to the surface. These\ngusty winds are expected to continue for the next few hours\nbefore quickly dying down this evening, and no changes have been\nmade to the ongoing WIND ADVISORY, which continues until 4 PM.\n\nAs the surface cold front over western Nebraska progresses\neastward overnight, clouds should increase over the entire CWA.\nAdditionally, there may be enough moisture convergence over\nportions of western IA between 06Z-09Z Saturday for a few\nshowers to develop. As a result, we have added slight chance \nPoPs (20%) over our far northeastern counties.\n\nThe cold front will pass through our CWA throughout the day\nSaturday. This will result in a wide range of afternoon high \ntemperatures across the area, with mid-40s near the NE/SD border\nand upper-60s in far southeast Nebraska. By afternoon, lift \nshould increase over the area as the right entrance region of \nthe 300-mb ULJ moves overhead and the associated upper-level \ntrough moves east from the Rockies. This, combined with \npersistent WAA ahead of the front should allow for an area of \nrain to develop Saturday afternoon over the southeastern 2/3rds\nof the CWA and intensify during the evening (60-100% PoPs). The\nheaviest rainfall amounts will likely be found in our far \nsoutheastern counties, where a few rumbles of thunder cannot be\nruled out due to forecast MUCAPE values on the order of a few\nhundred J/kg. The front and associated rain will pass south/east\nof our area by early Sunday morning.\n\nBehind the front, a seasonably cool airmass will settle into the\nupper Midwest by Sunday, with temperatures expected to be \nroughly 5-10 degrees below normal into next week. Flow aloft\nshould remain largely zonal, with the only exception being a\nweak upper-level disturbance passing just to our south on\nTuesday. However, this system is not expected to produce any\nprecipitation, and on the whole, conditions are expected to be\nquiet through at least Thanksgiving Day.",
